#---
# purpose: "Enumerable.reduce/3 suspend returns a real resumable closure at the runtime-value boundary"
#---
#
# # enum_reduce_suspend
#
# Pins the materialization boundary for `Enumerable.reduce_list/3` suspend:
#
# ```fz
# Enumerable.reduce(xs, {:suspend, 0}, fn (x, acc) -> {:cont, acc + x})
# ```
#
# The suspend clause returns `{:suspended, acc, fn () -> ... end}`. That closure
# is a source value, not an internal native continuation edge, so it remains a
# real heap closure even as native call continuations become direct or lazy.
#
# Current contract, native `run`/`build`:
#
# - the three-element input list allocates three cons cells;
# - the returned suspend function allocates one closure (`closure_allocs = 1`,
#   `closure_bytes = 48`);
# - one protocol-control struct is allocated (`struct_allocs = 1`,
#   `struct_bytes = 48`);
# - the accumulator is never boxed (`scalar_box_allocs = 0`);
# - the rendered function identity is `#fn<4/0>`.
#
# Current contract, `interp`:
#
# - same list-cons count as native;
# - the suspended continuation is a real heap closure, same as native, but
#   `interp` allocates a second closure (`closure_allocs = 2`,
#   `closure_bytes = 64`) and two protocol-control structs
#   (`struct_allocs = 2`, `struct_bytes = 80`);
# - the rendered function identity is `#fn<3/0>`. The arity matches native's
#   because arity is a property of the source (`fn () -> ... end` takes no
#   arguments); the leading env-schema id differs because schema ids are
#   assigned in allocation order, which is not a semantic difference in the
#   suspend boundary.
fn main() do
  xs = [1, 2, 3]
  out = Enumerable.reduce(xs, {:suspend, 0}, fn (x, acc) -> {:cont, acc + x} end)

  dbg(out)
  stats = Process.heap_alloc_stats()
  dbg({
    stats[:list_cons_allocs],
    stats[:list_cons_bytes],
    stats[:struct_allocs],
    stats[:struct_bytes],
    stats[:closure_allocs],
    stats[:closure_bytes],
    stats[:scalar_box_allocs],
    stats[:scalar_box_bytes]
  })
end
